    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d107">

                <pb facs="0239" n="191" xml:id="pg191"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00/9–1352: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 107<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom
                            (Gifford) to
                        the Department of State</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d107-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-09-13T12:00:00+01:00">September 13,
                        1952—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1474. We do not believe Brit CSC policy as
                    described Embtel 1201 Aug 30<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d107-f2"/> is inconsistent with apparent confusion and vagueness
                    Brit attitude as reported Strasbourg tel to Dept 46, Sept 10.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d107-f3"/> We believe Brit have reluctantly accepted that (1)
                    growing European integration is both inevitable and desirable in over-all west
                    interest and (2) Brit cannot participate on supra-natl basis. Their basic
                    approach to prob is therefore to attempt to guide developments so that they have
                    maximum influence with minimum commitments. They apparently think best way to
                    achieve this is to maintain deliberately vague position (they continually stress
                    “flexibility” of Eden plan) even to
                    point of embarrassing their officials in the field. We feel confident, however,
                    that they will make the best of any arrangements, even though they do not
                    obviously and unfairly restrict their participation in developments on basis
                    above two fundamental points.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Gifford</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
